---
import { reportData, round } from '../../../site/src/lib/report-data';
const { section } = Astro.props;
const data = await reportData('exp169', { allowUnavailable: true });
---
{section === "abstract" && (data ? <p>We inherited trained COBA and PING classifiers and kept their weights fixed. We measured the strength and variability of the inhibition received by excitatory (E) neurons in PING, then built artificial GABA inputs matching those properties and delivered them asynchronously to COBA. For the same digit image, mean E firing fell from {round(data.conditions[0].rate_e_hz, 1)} to {round(data.conditions[2].rate_e_hz, 1)} Hz—about {round(100*(1-data.conditions[2].rate_e_hz/data.conditions[0].rate_e_hz), 0)}% suppression. This single-image result shows that matched asynchronous inhibition can almost eliminate COBA's excitatory activity, although the artificial event rate differed from PING's.</p> : <p>No completed evidence is selected.</p>)}
{section === "onset" && (data?.onset_comparison ? <p>Establishing only the artificial inhibitory conductance before image onset reduces E firing over 0–10 ms from {round(data.onset_comparison.zero_start.rate_e_0_10_ms_hz, 2)} to {round(data.onset_comparison.artificial.rate_e_0_10_ms_hz, 2)} Hz. Over 50–200 ms, firing is {round(data.onset_comparison.zero_start.rate_e_50_200_ms_hz, 2)} and {round(data.onset_comparison.artificial.rate_e_50_200_ms_hz, 2)} Hz, respectively. This paired comparison holds the scored sensory input, artificial events, amplitudes and weights fixed. Figure 3 shows the pre-established condition.</p> : null)}
